</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Department of Computer Science and Engineering, Innopolis University, Russia 
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>MB3R Lab, Innopolis, 420500, Russia
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Mechanistic interpretability has identified functional subgraphs within large language models (LLMs), known as Transformer Circuits (TCs), that appear to implement specific algorithms. Yet we lack a formal, single-pass way to quantify when an <em id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">active</em> circuit is behaving coherently and thus likely trustworthy. Building on prior systems-theoretic proposals, we specialize a sheaf/cohomology and causal emergence perspective to TCs and introduce the <em id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Effective-Information Consistency Score (EICS)</em>. EICS combines (i) a <em id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">normalized sheaf inconsistency</em> computed from local Jacobians and activations, with (ii) a <em id="abstract1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Gaussian EI proxy</em> for circuit-level causal emergence derived from the same forward state. The construction is white-box, single-pass, and makes units explicit so that the score is dimensionless. We further provide practical guidance on score interpretation, computational overhead (with fast and exact modes), and a toy sanity-check analysis. Empirical validation on LLM tasks is deferred.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Keywords: </h6>Mechanistic interpretability Transformer circuits Sheaf theory Causal emergence Uncertainty quantification Large Language Models (LLMs)
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A central ambition in mechanistic interpretability is to reverse-engineer LLMs into meso-scale algorithmic components (“transformer circuits”) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>. These subgraphs (attention heads, MLPs, and their pathways) have been linked to tasks such as copy/induction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> and factual recall <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>. Once a circuit is identified, a natural question is: <em id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">is it functioning coherently on this input?</em> The same model may answer a factual prompt correctly or hallucinate; we hypothesize that the active circuit’s <em id="S1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">causal integrity</em> differs between these regimes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We build on sheaf-theoretic and causal-emergence ideas <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>, instantiating them for transformer circuits. Our contribution is an <em id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">operational</em> metric, EICS, that can be computed from a single forward pass. Conceptually, uncertainty is treated as a failure of causal integrity: high emergence with low internal inconsistency suggests confidence; the opposite suggests risk. Unlike black-box UQ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>, EICS attributes uncertainty to specific <em id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">mechanisms</em>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Mechanistic interpretability.</span> Induction heads and in-context learning behaviors were documented by Olsson et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>. Recent “attribution graphs” tools (a.k.a. circuit tracing) provide subgraph discovery and validation pipelines <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>. Knowledge-related circuits are actively studied <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>, and the relation between localization and editing has been scrutinized <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Uncertainty quantification (UQ).</span> Black-box UQ includes distribution-free conformal prediction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>, calibration post-processing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>, deep ensembles <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>, and Bayesian/approximate Bayesian methods for LLM finetuning such as Laplace-LoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>. Our aim is complementary: a <em id="S2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">white-box</em> signal grounded in the internal circuit.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sheaves and causal emergence.</span> Cellular sheaves provide a language for stitching local linear maps into globally consistent states; disagreement is measured via coboundaries and Hodge Laplacians <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>. Causal emergence quantifies when macroscale descriptions carry more effective information than parts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>. We adapt these ingredients to transformer circuits with explicit, computable proxies.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Preliminaries</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Transformer circuits</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let the transformer be a directed acyclic graph (DAG) <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=(V,E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=(V,E)</annotation></semantics></math> with vertices as heads/MLPs and edges for information flow. A TC is a subgraph <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{M}\subseteq G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo>⊆</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{M}\subseteq G</annotation></semantics></math> hypothesized to implement a task. For an input <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, each <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V_{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>M</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V_{M}</annotation></semantics></math> has activation <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{v}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{v}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{v}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{v}}</annotation></semantics></math>. We will write <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e=(u\!\to\!v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e=(u\!\to\!v)</annotation></semantics></math> for an oriented edge and use local linearizations at the observed activations.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Cellular sheaves on graphs and a computable inconsistency</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We place a cellular sheaf <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> on the <em id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">underlying undirected</em> version of <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{M}</annotation></semantics></math> (so that 1-cochains live on edges regardless of the DAG’s direction), with stalks <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}(v)=\mathbb{R}^{d_{v}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}(v)=\mathbb{R}^{d_{v}}</annotation></semantics></math> and restriction maps on oriented edges given by the Jacobians evaluated at the current state:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{e}:\mathcal{F}(u)\to\mathcal{F}(v),\qquad\rho_{u\to v}\;:=\;J_{u\to v}\;\equiv\;\Big(\frac{\partial f_{v}}{\partial a_{u}}\Big)_{a}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.558em">:=</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.558em">≡</mo><msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{e}:\mathcal{F}(u)\to\mathcal{F}(v),\qquad\rho_{u\to v}\;:=\;J_{u\to v}\;\equiv\;\Big(\frac{\partial f_{v}}{\partial a_{u}}\Big)_{a}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">For a 0-cochain (node assignment) <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=\{s_{v}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=\{s_{v}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, the sheaf coboundary <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta^{0}:\!C^{0}\!\to\!C^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>δ</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.108em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo rspace="0.108em" stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta^{0}:\!C^{0}\!\to\!C^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> acts by</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\delta^{0}s)_{(u\to v)}\;=\;\rho_{u\to v}\,s_{u}\;-\;s_{v}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>δ</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.502em">−</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\delta^{0}s)_{(u\to v)}\;=\;\rho_{u\to v}\,s_{u}\;-\;s_{v}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">On a graph (no 2-cells), <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta^{1}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta^{1}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, hence <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{1}\cong C^{1}/\mathrm{im}\,\delta^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>im</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{1}\cong C^{1}/\mathrm{im}\,\delta^{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. Rather than taking a non-canonical “norm of a quotient,” we use a <em id="S3.SS2.p1.3.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">normalized inconsistency energy</em> from the observed activations <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=\{a_{v}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=\{a_{v}\}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\mathrm{sh}}(G_{M},a)\;=\;\frac{\left(\sum_{(u\to v)\in E_{M}}\|\rho_{u\to v}a_{u}-a_{v}\|_{2}^{2}\right)^{1/2}}{\varepsilon+\left(\sum_{(u\to v)\in E_{M}}\|a_{u}\|_{2}^{2}+\|a_{v}\|_{2}^{2}\right)^{1/2}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>sh</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>M</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>M</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\mathrm{sh}}(G_{M},a)\;=\;\frac{\left(\sum_{(u\to v)\in E_{M}}\|\rho_{u\to v}a_{u}-a_{v}\|_{2}^{2}\right)^{1/2}}{\varepsilon+\left(\sum_{(u\to v)\in E_{M}}\|a_{u}\|_{2}^{2}+\|a_{v}\|_{2}^{2}\right)^{1/2}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.4" class="ltx_p">with small <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> for numerical stability. This is dimensionless and equals <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> iff <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> is a (noisily) consistent global section. One can optionally replace <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> by the least-squares projection <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{s}=\arg\min_{s}\sum_{e}\|\rho_{e}s_{u}-s_{v}\|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mmultiscripts><mo>∑</mo><mi>e</mi><mrow></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mi>s</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em">⁡</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{s}=\arg\min_{s}\sum_{e}\|\rho_{e}s_{u}-s_{v}\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>; both are single-pass (VJP/JVP-based) computations.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Node-seeded JVP computation (efficiency).</h5>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate (<a href="#S3.E2" title="In 3.2 Cellular sheaves on graphs and a computable inconsistency ‣ 3 Preliminaries ‣ Measuring Uncertainty in Transformer Circuits with Effective Information Consistency" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) efficiently, we use a <em id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">node-seeded</em> JVP scheme: for each source node <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in V_{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>M</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in V_{M}</annotation></semantics></math> we perform a single JVP with seed <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> to evaluate all outgoing residual terms <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{u\to v}a_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{u\to v}a_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> in one pass. This reduces the complexity from per-edge JVPs to <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(|V_{M}|)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(|V_{M}|)</annotation></semantics></math> JVPs (typically <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>20</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">20</annotation></semantics></math> for medium circuits), while preserving the exact definition in (<a href="#S3.E2" title="In 3.2 Cellular sheaves on graphs and a computable inconsistency ‣ 3 Preliminaries ‣ Measuring Uncertainty in Transformer Circuits with Effective Information Consistency" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>A single-pass, Gaussian EI proxy</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">True effective information (EI) is intervention-defined. To obtain a single-pass proxy, we assume small, isotropic local interventions at the current state and approximate each map by its Jacobian. For a linear map <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=Jx+\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=Jx+\xi</annotation></semantics></math> with unit-variance isotropic <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and small additive noise <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ξ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi</annotation></semantics></math>, the mutual information (in nats) is proportional to <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tfrac{1}{2}\log\det(I+\alpha J^{\top}J)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">det</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>J</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tfrac{1}{2}\log\det(I+\alpha J^{\top}J)</annotation></semantics></math> for scale <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. We therefore define</p>
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{EI}_{G}(J)\;:=\;\tfrac{1}{2}\log\det\!\big(I+\alpha J^{\top}J\big),\qquad\Delta\mathrm{EI}_{G}(G_{M})\;:=\;\mathrm{EI}_{G}(J_{M})\;-\;\sum_{v\in V_{M}}\mathrm{EI}_{G}(J_{v})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>EI</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>J</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">:=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.177em;" width="1.177em"><mo movablelimits="false">det</mo></mpadded><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>J</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>EI</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>EI</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.502em">−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>M</mi></msub></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>EI</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{EI}_{G}(J)\;:=\;\tfrac{1}{2}\log\det\!\big(I+\alpha J^{\top}J\big),\qquad\Delta\mathrm{EI}_{G}(G_{M})\;:=\;\mathrm{EI}_{G}(J_{M})\;-\;\sum_{v\in V_{M}}\mathrm{EI}_{G}(J_{v}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{M}</annotation></semantics></math> is the macro-Jacobian from the circuit’s inputs to its outputs (obtained by linearizing the composed subgraph). We use the positive part <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\mathrm{EI}_{G}^{+}=\max(0,\Delta\mathrm{EI}_{G})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>EI</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>EI</mi><mi>G</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\mathrm{EI}_{G}^{+}=\max(0,\Delta\mathrm{EI}_{G})</annotation></semantics></math>, and an optional normalization <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\Delta\mathrm{EI}}_{G}:=\Delta\mathrm{EI}_{G}^{+}/\big(\varepsilon+\mathrm{EI}_{G}(J_{M})\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>EI</mi></mrow><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>EI</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>EI</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Delta\mathrm{EI}}_{G}:=\Delta\mathrm{EI}_{G}^{+}/\big(\varepsilon+\mathrm{EI}_{G}(J_{M})\big)</annotation></semantics></math> to keep scores in <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4 </span>Gaussian EI Proxy — Derivation and Implementation Notes</h3>

<section id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Setup.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consider a local, linearized description of a circuit around the observed forward state. Let <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> denote a small stochastic intervention at the circuit inputs, and let <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in\mathbb{R}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in\mathbb{R}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> be the circuit outputs. We approximate</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\;=\;J\,x+\xi,\qquad x\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_{x}^{2}I_{n}),\ \ \xi\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_{\xi}^{2}I_{m})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>ξ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\;=\;J\,x+\xi,\qquad x\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_{x}^{2}I_{n}),\ \ \xi\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_{\xi}^{2}I_{m}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Mutual information.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For a linear Gaussian channel with independent Gaussian input and noise,</p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(x;y)\;=\;\tfrac{1}{2}\log\det\!\Big(I_{m}+\tfrac{\sigma_{x}^{2}}{\sigma_{\xi}^{2}}JJ^{\top}\Big)\;=\;\tfrac{1}{2}\log\det\!\Big(I_{n}+\tfrac{\sigma_{x}^{2}}{\sigma_{\xi}^{2}}J^{\top}J\Big)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.177em;" width="1.177em"><mo movablelimits="false">det</mo></mpadded><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>ξ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>J</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>J</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" rspace="0.280em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.177em;" width="1.177em"><mo movablelimits="false">det</mo></mpadded><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>ξ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>J</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(x;y)\;=\;\tfrac{1}{2}\log\det\!\Big(I_{m}+\tfrac{\sigma_{x}^{2}}{\sigma_{\xi}^{2}}JJ^{\top}\Big)\;=\;\tfrac{1}{2}\log\det\!\Big(I_{n}+\tfrac{\sigma_{x}^{2}}{\sigma_{\xi}^{2}}J^{\top}J\Big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Proxy definition.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use the Gaussian EI proxy from Eq. (<a href="#S3.E3" title="In 3.3 A single-pass, Gaussian EI proxy ‣ 3 Preliminaries ‣ Measuring Uncertainty in Transformer Circuits with Effective Information Consistency" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>); for a circuit <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{M}</annotation></semantics></math>
the emergence and its normalized positive part are, as in §3.3,</p>
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\Delta\mathrm{EI}}_{G}\;=\;\frac{\max(0,\Delta\mathrm{EI}_{G})}{\varepsilon+\mathrm{EI}_{G}(J_{M})}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>EI</mi></mrow><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>EI</mi><mi>G</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>EI</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Delta\mathrm{EI}}_{G}\;=\;\frac{\max(0,\Delta\mathrm{EI}_{G})}{\varepsilon+\mathrm{EI}_{G}(J_{M})}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Invariance, sensitivity, and small-<math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> approximation.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{EI}_{G}(J)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>EI</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{EI}_{G}(J)</annotation></semantics></math> depends only on the singular values of <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math>. Its sensitivity to <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is</p>
<table id="S3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial}{\partial\alpha}\Big(\tfrac{1}{2}\log\det(I+\alpha J^{\top}J)\Big)=\tfrac{1}{2}\,\mathrm{tr}\!\big[(I+\alpha J^{\top}J)^{-1}J^{\top}J\big]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">det</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>J</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mpadded style="width:0.670em;" width="0.670em"><mi>tr</mi></mpadded><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>J</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>J</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial}{\partial\alpha}\Big(\tfrac{1}{2}\log\det(I+\alpha J^{\top}J)\Big)=\tfrac{1}{2}\,\mathrm{tr}\!\big[(I+\alpha J^{\top}J)^{-1}J^{\top}J\big],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">and for <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\,\sigma_{\max}^{2}\ll 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>max</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>≪</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\,\sigma_{\max}^{2}\ll 1</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S3.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tfrac{1}{2}\log\det(I+\alpha J^{\top}J)\;\approx\;\tfrac{\alpha}{2}\,\|J\|_{F}^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">det</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>J</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≈</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tfrac{1}{2}\log\det(I+\alpha J^{\top}J)\;\approx\;\tfrac{\alpha}{2}\,\|J\|_{F}^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Computation.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Use Cholesky/eigendecomposition for small <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>; for larger <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, use Hutch++/Lanczos log-det estimators with only JVP/VJP products. Residual connections can be handled by building the linearized block operator or evaluating log-det via Krylov methods.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Method: EICS</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, activations <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>, and edge Jacobians <math id="S4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\rho_{u\to v}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\rho_{u\to v}\}</annotation></semantics></math> from a single forward pass, we define</p>
<table id="S4.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{EICS}(G_{M};a)\;=\;\frac{\widetilde{\Delta\mathrm{EI}}_{G}(G_{M})}{1+C_{\mathrm{sh}}(G_{M},a)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>EICS</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>EI</mi></mrow><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>sh</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{EICS}(G_{M};a)\;=\;\frac{\widetilde{\Delta\mathrm{EI}}_{G}(G_{M})}{1+C_{\mathrm{sh}}(G_{M},a)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">High EICS indicates (i) strong macro-level information integration relative to parts and (ii) low internal disagreement across edges.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Why this fixes earlier issues.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">(1) We never take a norm of a quotient space <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>; we measure <em id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">disagreement energy</em> (<a href="#S3.E2" title="In 3.2 Cellular sheaves on graphs and a computable inconsistency ‣ 3 Preliminaries ‣ Measuring Uncertainty in Transformer Circuits with Effective Information Consistency" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) directly and dimensionlessly. (2) The EI term is a clearly stated Gaussian-logdet proxy; units are nats and become dimensionless via normalization. (3) DAGs have no directed cycles, but sheaf inconsistency remains meaningful on the undirected 1-skeleton.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSSx1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Practical use and interpretation.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">By construction <math id="S4.SS0.SSSx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\mathrm{sh}}\!\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>sh</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.108em">≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\mathrm{sh}}\!\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS0.SSSx1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\Delta\mathrm{EI}}_{G}\in[0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>EI</mi></mrow><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Delta\mathrm{EI}}_{G}\in[0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>, hence <math id="S4.SS0.SSSx1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{EICS}\in[0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>EICS</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{EICS}\in[0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>. Use a development split to select a threshold <math id="S4.SS0.SSSx1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> (AUROC/F1) and also report the components <math id="S4.SS0.SSSx1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/(1+C_{\mathrm{sh}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>sh</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/(1+C_{\mathrm{sh}})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS0.SSSx1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\Delta\mathrm{EI}}_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>EI</mi></mrow><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Delta\mathrm{EI}}_{G}</annotation></semantics></math> to diagnose drivers. For <math id="S4.SS0.SSSx1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, either set <math id="S4.SS0.SSSx1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=1</annotation></semantics></math> (SNR prior) or choose <math id="S4.SS0.SSSx1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> to keep <math id="S4.SS0.SSSx1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tfrac{1}{2}\log\det(I+\alpha J_{M}^{\top}J_{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">det</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>J</mi><mi>M</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>M</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tfrac{1}{2}\log\det(I+\alpha J_{M}^{\top}J_{M})</annotation></semantics></math> in a target IQR to avoid saturation.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Theoretical properties</h2>

<div id="Thmassumption1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmassumption1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 1</span></span><span id="Thmassumption1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> (Local linearity and boundedness)</span></h6>
<div id="Thmassumption1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmassumption1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmassumption1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Along <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, the maps are locally linear with Jacobians <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\rho_{e}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\rho_{e}\}</annotation></semantics></math> Lipschitz in a neighborhood of <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>, and operator norms are bounded. The sheaf Hodge Laplacian <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="L=\delta^{0\dagger}\delta^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>δ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">†</mo></mrow></msup><mi>δ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=\delta^{0\dagger}\delta^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> (with the inner product induced by edge weights) has spectral gap <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{2}(L)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{2}(L)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="Thmassumption1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">[</span><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a><span id="Thmassumption1.p1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">]</span></cite>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmproposition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproposition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 1</span></span><span id="Thmproposition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> (Single-pass computability)</span></h6>
<div id="Thmproposition1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproposition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproposition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Under Assumption <a href="#Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 1 (Local linearity and boundedness) ‣ 5 Theoretical properties ‣ Measuring Uncertainty in Transformer Circuits with Effective Information Consistency" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, both <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\mathrm{sh}}(G_{M},a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>sh</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\mathrm{sh}}(G_{M},a)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\Delta\mathrm{EI}}_{G}(G_{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>EI</mi></mrow><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Delta\mathrm{EI}}_{G}(G_{M})</annotation></semantics></math> are deterministic functions of a single forward pass and its Jacobian-vector products. Consequently, <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{EICS}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>EICS</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{EICS}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(1)</annotation></semantics></math> in the number of forward passes.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Sketch.</span> <math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>δ</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is built from <math id="S5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\rho_{e}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\rho_{e}\}</annotation></semantics></math> evaluated at <math id="S5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>. Both the residual (<a href="#S3.E2" title="In 3.2 Cellular sheaves on graphs and a computable inconsistency ‣ 3 Preliminaries ‣ Measuring Uncertainty in Transformer Circuits with Effective Information Consistency" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) and log-det terms (<a href="#S3.E3" title="In 3.3 A single-pass, Gaussian EI proxy ‣ 3 Preliminaries ‣ Measuring Uncertainty in Transformer Circuits with Effective Information Consistency" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) are functions of these objects. No Monte Carlo over inputs is required.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmproposition2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproposition2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2</span></span><span id="Thmproposition2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> (Stability to small off-circuit perturbations (bound))</span></h6>
<div id="Thmproposition2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproposition2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproposition2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmproposition2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> denote circuit outputs. Consider an additive off-circuit perturbation <math id="Thmproposition2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> that couples into <math id="Thmproposition2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{M}</annotation></semantics></math> with gain at most <math id="Thmproposition2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> in operator norm. Under Assumption <a href="#Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 1 (Local linearity and boundedness) ‣ 5 Theoretical properties ‣ Measuring Uncertainty in Transformer Circuits with Effective Information Consistency" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>,</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\hat{s}-s^{\star}\|\;\leq\;\frac{\gamma}{\lambda_{2}(L)}\;\|\eta\|,\qquad\|\Delta y\|\leq\kappa\|\hat{s}-s^{\star}\|" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>γ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.280em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\hat{s}-s^{\star}\|\;\leq\;\frac{\gamma}{\lambda_{2}(L)}\;\|\eta\|,\qquad\|\Delta y\|\leq\kappa\|\hat{s}-s^{\star}\|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmproposition2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproposition2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">for a local Lipschitz constant <math id="Thmproposition2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, small <math id="Thmproposition2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\mathrm{sh}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>sh</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\mathrm{sh}}</annotation></semantics></math> (implying a large effective <math id="Thmproposition2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{2}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{2}(L)</annotation></semantics></math>) yields tighter bounds.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">On the role of <math id="S5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{2}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{2}(L)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span>
The bound scales with <math id="S5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\lambda_{2}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\lambda_{2}(L)</annotation></semantics></math> (Fiedler value). In practice <math id="S5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{2}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{2}(L)</annotation></semantics></math> depends on (i) connectivity and (ii) edge weights induced by local Jacobians. We recommend: (a) <em id="S5.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">report</em> <math id="S5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{2}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{2}(L)</annotation></semantics></math> per circuit; (b) <em id="S5.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">normalize edge weights</em> by operator norms of <math id="S5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{u\to v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{u\to v}</annotation></semantics></math>; and (c) optionally regularize <math id="S5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\leftarrow L+\beta I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\leftarrow L+\beta I</annotation></semantics></math> when empirical <math id="S5.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{2}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{2}(L)</annotation></semantics></math> is near-zero—tightening the practical bound without changing <math id="S5.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\mathrm{sh}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>sh</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\mathrm{sh}}</annotation></semantics></math> or EICS.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Algorithm</h2>

<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> Single-pass EICS for a transformer circuit</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
            
            
          <span id="alg1.l1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input:</span> Model <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, input <math id="alg1.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, circuit <math id="alg1.l1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{M}=(V_{M},E_{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{M}=(V_{M},E_{M})</annotation></semantics></math>, scale <math id="alg1.l1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
            
            
          <span id="alg1.l2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Output:</span> <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{EICS}(G_{M};a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>EICS</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{EICS}(G_{M};a)</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
            
            
          <span id="alg1.l3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Forward &amp; activations:</span> Run <math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> and record <math id="alg1.l3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{a_{v}\}_{v\in V_{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>M</mi></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{a_{v}\}_{v\in V_{M}}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
            
            
          <span id="alg1.l4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Edge Jacobians:</span> For each <math id="alg1.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(u\to v)\in E_{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>M</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(u\to v)\in E_{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, compute <math id="alg1.l4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{u\to v}=\big(\partial f_{v}/\partial a_{u}\big)_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em">/</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{u\to v}=\big(\partial f_{v}/\partial a_{u}\big)_{a}</annotation></semantics></math> via VJP/JVP.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
            
            
          <span id="alg1.l5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sheaf inconsistency:</span> Compute <math id="alg1.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\mathrm{sh}}(G_{M},a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>sh</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\mathrm{sh}}(G_{M},a)</annotation></semantics></math> using (<a href="#S3.E2" title="In 3.2 Cellular sheaves on graphs and a computable inconsistency ‣ 3 Preliminaries ‣ Measuring Uncertainty in Transformer Circuits with Effective Information Consistency" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>). <em id="alg1.l5.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Implementation:</em> Use <em id="alg1.l5.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">node-seeded JVPs</em> (one JVP per source node <math id="alg1.l5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math>) to evaluate all <math id="alg1.l5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{u\to v}a_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{u\to v}a_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> for outgoing edges.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
            
            
          <span id="alg1.l6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Gaussian EI proxy:</span> Build macro-Jacobian <math id="alg1.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{M}</annotation></semantics></math> and node Jacobians <math id="alg1.l6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{J_{v}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{J_{v}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Compute <math id="alg1.l6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\Delta\mathrm{EI}}_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>EI</mi></mrow><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Delta\mathrm{EI}}_{G}</annotation></semantics></math> via (<a href="#S3.E3" title="In 3.3 A single-pass, Gaussian EI proxy ‣ 3 Preliminaries ‣ Measuring Uncertainty in Transformer Circuits with Effective Information Consistency" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>).

</div>
<div id="alg1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">7:</span></span>
            
            
           <span id="alg1.l7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Fast mode (ranking):</span> small-<math id="alg1.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> approximation; Hutch++/Lanczos with 4–8 probes per <math id="alg1.l7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> and 8–12 for <math id="alg1.l7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{M}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">8:</span></span>
            
            
           <span id="alg1.l8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Exact mode (small blocks):</span> compute <math id="alg1.l8.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\log\det(I+\alpha J^{\top}J)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">det</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>α</mi><msup><mi>J</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log\det(I+\alpha J^{\top}J)</annotation></semantics></math> via Cholesky/SVD.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l9.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">9:</span></span>
            
            
          <span id="alg1.l9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Score:</span> Return <math id="alg1.l9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{EICS}=\widetilde{\Delta\mathrm{EI}}_{G}/(1+C_{\mathrm{sh}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>EICS</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>EI</mi></mrow><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>sh</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{EICS}=\widetilde{\Delta\mathrm{EI}}_{G}/(1+C_{\mathrm{sh}})</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
</div>
</figure>
<section id="S6.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Computational overhead &amp; scaling</h3>

<div id="S6.SSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SSx1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Order-of-magnitude.</span> With node-seeded JVPs, computing <math id="S6.SSx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\mathrm{sh}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>sh</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\mathrm{sh}}</annotation></semantics></math> requires <math id="S6.SSx1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(|V_{M}|)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(|V_{M}|)</annotation></semantics></math> JVPs (about <math id="S6.SSx1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S6.SSx1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>20</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">20</annotation></semantics></math> for medium circuits). In <em id="S6.SSx1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">fast</em> EI mode (small-<math id="S6.SSx1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> Frobenius approximation + Hutchinson-style probes), total autograd work is typically <math id="S6.SSx1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim 50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mn>50</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim 50</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S6.SSx1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="200" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>200</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">200</annotation></semantics></math> JVP/VJP products over the restricted subgraph, or about <math id="S6.SSx1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim 2</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S6.SSx1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6</annotation></semantics></math> forward-equivalents. In <em id="S6.SSx1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">exact</em> EI mode (larger <math id="S6.SSx1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> or explicit factorizations), expect <math id="S6.SSx1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim 5</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S6.SSx1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>15</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">15</annotation></semantics></math> forward-equivalents. There is no input-level Monte Carlo; all quantities are deterministic functions of one forward state.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SSx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SSx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SSx1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Scaling knobs.</span> (a) Batch JVP seeds across nodes; (b) restrict EI to top-<math id="S6.SSx1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> singular directions via Lanczos; (c) cache intermediate linears along the TC; (d) prefer small-<math id="S6.SSx1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> for ranking tasks.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Proposed Validation</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We outline an evaluation protocol for a factual QA task using an attribution-graph workflow <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> to identify a fact-retrieval circuit <math id="S7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{\mathrm{fact}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>fact</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{\mathrm{fact}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Two sets are proposed: (A) questions with verifiable answers; (B) adversarial or hallucination-inducing prompts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>. The expectation is higher <math id="S7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{EICS}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>EICS</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{EICS}</annotation></semantics></math> for set A (low <math id="S7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\mathrm{sh}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>sh</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\mathrm{sh}}</annotation></semantics></math>, positive <math id="S7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\Delta\mathrm{EI}}_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>EI</mi></mrow><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Delta\mathrm{EI}}_{G}</annotation></semantics></math>) and depressed scores for set B. Comparators include log-prob/entropy, deep-ensemble variance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>, and conformal set sizes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S7.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.1 </span>Baselines and ablations</h3>

<div id="S7.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(B1) Edge Activation Correlation (EAC).</span> Average Pearson correlation between <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> across <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(u\!\to\!v)\in E_{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>M</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(u\!\to\!v)\in E_{M}</annotation></semantics></math> (dimension-wise). 
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S7.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(B2) Edge Alignment Residual (EAR).</span> <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{|E_{M}|}\sum_{(u\to v)}\|\hat{\rho}_{u\to v}a_{u}-a_{v}\|_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{|E_{M}|}\sum_{(u\to v)}\|\hat{\rho}_{u\to v}a_{u}-a_{v}\|_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> with per-edge least-squares <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\rho}_{u\to v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\rho}_{u\to v}</annotation></semantics></math> (no sheaf coupling). 
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S7.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Ablations.</span> (A1) <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/(1+C_{\mathrm{sh}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>sh</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/(1+C_{\mathrm{sh}})</annotation></semantics></math> only; (A2) <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\Delta\mathrm{EI}}_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>EI</mi></mrow><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Delta\mathrm{EI}}_{G}</annotation></semantics></math> only.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.2 </span>Toy sanity-check (analytical &amp; simulation protocol)</h3>

<div id="S7.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Setup.</span> A 6-node feedforward TC with linear blocks and additive Gaussian edge noise; vary noise on a subset of edges. Compute <math id="S7.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\mathrm{sh}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>sh</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\mathrm{sh}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\Delta\mathrm{EI}}_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>EI</mi></mrow><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Delta\mathrm{EI}}_{G}</annotation></semantics></math> (fast/exact), EICS, and baselines across seeds.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Analytical sanity (small-<math id="S7.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>).</span> With <math id="S7.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=\sigma_{x}^{2}/\sigma_{\xi}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>ξ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=\sigma_{x}^{2}/\sigma_{\xi}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, increasing additive noise reduces EI terms; edge noise increases residuals in (<a href="#S3.E2" title="In 3.2 Cellular sheaves on graphs and a computable inconsistency ‣ 3 Preliminaries ‣ Measuring Uncertainty in Transformer Circuits with Effective Information Consistency" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>). Hence EICS decreases with noise, matching intuition. See Fig. <a href="#S7.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 7.2 Toy sanity-check (analytical &amp; simulation protocol) ‣ 7 Proposed Validation ‣ Measuring Uncertainty in Transformer Circuits with Effective Information Consistency" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> for a synthetic sanity-check illustrating these trends.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S7.F1" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.07149v1/fig_toy_noise_curve.svg" id="S7.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:429/286;" width="429" height="286"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Toy sanity-check on a 6-node circuit with two parallel branches. As node-noise <math id="S7.F1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> increases, the sheaf inconsistency <math id="S7.F1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\mathrm{sh}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>sh</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\mathrm{sh}}</annotation></semantics></math> rises (so <math id="S7.F1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/(1+C_{\mathrm{sh}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>sh</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/(1+C_{\mathrm{sh}})</annotation></semantics></math> falls). We also reduce cross-branch alignment with <math id="S7.F1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> (edge decoherence), causing the emergence proxy <math id="S7.F1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\Delta\mathrm{EI}}_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>EI</mi></mrow><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Delta\mathrm{EI}}_{G}</annotation></semantics></math> and the overall EICS to decrease. Curves show means over seeds (no error bands for clarity). Definitions follow Eqs. (<a href="#S3.E2" title="In 3.2 Cellular sheaves on graphs and a computable inconsistency ‣ 3 Preliminaries ‣ Measuring Uncertainty in Transformer Circuits with Effective Information Consistency" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), (<a href="#S3.E3" title="In 3.3 A single-pass, Gaussian EI proxy ‣ 3 Preliminaries ‣ Measuring Uncertainty in Transformer Circuits with Effective Information Consistency" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), and (<a href="#S4.E7" title="In 4 Method: EICS ‣ Measuring Uncertainty in Transformer Circuits with Effective Information Consistency" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>).</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S8" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">8 </span>Discussion &amp; limitations</h2>

<div id="S8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Circuit dependency.</span> EICS is only as meaningful as the specified <math id="S8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{M}</annotation></semantics></math>. <span id="S8.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Linearization.</span> The Jacobian-based sheaf and EI proxy assume local linearity. <span id="S8.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Cost.</span> Node-seeded JVPs, fast log-det, and top-<math id="S8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> directions mitigate overhead. <span id="S8.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Role with black-box UQ.</span> EICS provides <em id="S8.p1.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">mechanistic</em> evidence complementing calibration/conformal tools.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S9" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">9 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We instantiate a sheaf-theoretic and causal-emergence perspective on transformer circuits as a practical, single-pass score (EICS). By replacing ill-posed cohomology norms with a normalized disagreement energy and adopting a Gaussian log-det EI proxy, EICS is both computable and dimensionless. We add practical guidance, computational cost analysis with fast/exact modes, and a toy sanity-check; full empirical validation follows the proposed protocol.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Pt0.A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix 0.A </span>Toy Sanity-Check Code for Fig. <a href="#S7.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 7.2 Toy sanity-check (analytical &amp; simulation protocol) ‣ 7 Proposed Validation ‣ Measuring Uncertainty in Transformer Circuits with Effective Information Consistency" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a></h2>

<div id="Pt0.A1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Pt0.A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The script below reproduces Fig. <a href="#S7.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 7.2 Toy sanity-check (analytical &amp; simulation protocol) ‣ 7 Proposed Validation ‣ Measuring Uncertainty in Transformer Circuits with Effective Information Consistency" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. It implements the two-branch toy circuit, computes
<math id="Pt0.A1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\mathrm{sh}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>sh</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\mathrm{sh}}</annotation></semantics></math> (Eq. (<a href="#S3.E2" title="In 3.2 Cellular sheaves on graphs and a computable inconsistency ‣ 3 Preliminaries ‣ Measuring Uncertainty in Transformer Circuits with Effective Information Consistency" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>)), the normalized emergence proxy <math id="Pt0.A1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\Delta\mathrm{EI}}_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>EI</mi></mrow><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Delta\mathrm{EI}}_{G}</annotation></semantics></math>
(Eqs. (<a href="#S3.E3" title="In 3.3 A single-pass, Gaussian EI proxy ‣ 3 Preliminaries ‣ Measuring Uncertainty in Transformer Circuits with Effective Information Consistency" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>)), and <math id="Pt0.A1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{EICS}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>EICS</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{EICS}</annotation></semantics></math> (Eq. (<a href="#S4.E7" title="In 4 Method: EICS ‣ Measuring Uncertainty in Transformer Circuits with Effective Information Consistency" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>)) as functions of the noise scale <math id="Pt0.A1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Pt0.A1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<div id="Pt0.A1.p2.1" class="ltx_listing ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lst_numbers_left ltx_lstlisting ltx_framed ltx_framed_rectangle ltx_listing"><div class="ltx_listing_data"><a href="data:text/plain;base64," download="">⬇</a></div>
